You configure partitioning at the stream level in OLake, and the Iceberg writer creates the partitioned table structure [1].

**OLake UI:** configure your destination, select your table, open the **Partitioning** tab, add your partition field with its transform, then schedule a job [2].

**CLI / streams.json:** specify partitioning in your stream configuration using `partition_regex` [1]:

```json
{
  "selected_streams": {
    "my_namespace": [
      {
        "stream_name": "my_stream",
        "partition_regex": "/{timestamp_col, day}/{region, identity}"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Then run your sync as usual [1]. If a partition field is missing from a record, the writer sets it to `nil`, which Iceberg treats as null [1].

Note: the CLI section of the docs page is empty in the context, so the exact CLI flags aren't covered here — see the [partitioning page](https://olake.io/docs/writers/iceberg/partitioning/#configuration) for full details [2].

The S3 Parquet writer needs at least minimal S3 permissions to list, create, read and write in the bucket — the docs provide this sample IAM policy [1]:

```json
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Sid": "S3BucketReadWrite",
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": [
                "s3:ListBucket",
                "s3:GetBucket*",
                "s3:*Object"
            ],
            "Resource": [
                "arn:aws:s3:::{S3_BUCKET_NAME}",
                "arn:aws:s3:::{S3_BUCKET_NAME}/*"
            ]
        },
        {
            "Sid": "ListAllBuckets",
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": "s3:ListAllMyBuckets",
            "Resource": "*"
        }
    ]
}
```

If writes fail with `AccessDenied` / `403 Forbidden`, attach that policy to the IAM user or role, confirm the bucket ARN and bucket name match your destination config exactly (case-sensitive), and check that any STS session is still valid [2]. For MinIO or GCS, also ensure `S3Endpoint` is set in the destination config [2].

See the IAM Permissions page for full details [1].
